# Scenario-based, condensed. Book B: Linux. Covers Ch.1-8.
DECKS=[("SB","Linux for Cyber Ops - Scenarios (Ch.1-8)",[
("mc","You get a Linux shell and want your commands left out of history, and you notice '.' is early in the user's PATH. What do these two facts give you?",
 ["Hide tracks (unset HISTFILE / HISTSIZE=0 / set +o history) and hijack a command by dropping a fake binary that '.' in PATH runs first","Nothing useful","Only PATH matters, history can't be cleared","Only history matters, PATH is harmless"],0,
 "Ch.1: PATH is THE security env var; attackers also suppress bash history. A shell also behaves differently by how it's started (login vs not)."),
("sa","You find a second account in /etc/passwd with UID 0, and another account whose hash in /etc/shadow starts with '!'. What does each mean?",
 "UID 0 = root, so that second account is a hidden root backdoor (Linux authorizes by UID, not name). '!' in front of the hash = the account is locked.","Ch.2. Account files: passwd, shadow, group, gshadow. Expired password = change at login; expired account = can't log in at all."),
("mc","You crack a hash from /etc/shadow that starts with $6$. What is it, and how did John get the password?",
 ["SHA-512, found by repeated guessing (hash a guess, compare) - hashes are one-way, not reversible","MD5, reversed mathematically","Plaintext already","bcrypt, decrypted with the salt"],0,
 "Ch.3: $1$=MD5, $5$=SHA-256, $6$=SHA-512, $y$=yescrypt. Salt = same password hashes differently per account, so rainbow tables fail."),
("mc","You can edit the sshd PAM stack. How do you get a shell on every SSH login attempt, even failed ones?",
 ["Add pam_exec.so - it runs your program during authentication regardless of success","Add pam_pwhistory.so","Delete /etc/shadow","Change the login shell"],0,
 "Ch.3: pam_exec.so is the abuse; admins and attackers with privilege can edit PAM (/etc/pam.d)."),
("sa","An attacker logged in via SSH and wiped one login record, but you still caught the login. Name the 3 record systems and why wiping one isn't enough.",
 "utmp (who/w/last), lastlog (pam_lastlog.so), systemd (loginctl). Different programs write each, so scrubbing one leaves the login in the others.","Ch.4 (U-L-S). sudo is configured in /etc/sudoers via visudo; Polkit = elevation for the GUI."),
("mc","You see a process named 'nginx' using 100% CPU but the box doesn't run a web server. How do you confirm it's really an attacker's renamed binary?",
 ["Check /proc/<pid>/exe - it points at the real file on disk no matter what the process is named","You can't - the name is authoritative","Reboot and see if it returns","Check its PID number"],0,
 "Ch.5: defenders hunt by name, so attackers rename; /proc/<pid>/exe defeats it. Talk to a process with signals: SIGKILL(9) can't be caught, SIGTERM(15) can."),
("mc","An attacker wants persistence that survives reboot on a systemd box. Where do they put it, and where do you look?",
 ["A malicious .service unit or timer in /etc/systemd/system; check systemctl list-unit-files --state=enabled and list-timers","In bash history","In /tmp only","Nowhere - systemd can't persist malware"],0,
 "Ch.5: systemd is PID 1; admins and attackers both use it. "),
("sa","You locked yourself out of a Linux box (lost root password) and separately find a SUID-root binary you don't recognize. What can you do with each?",
 "Lost password: boot rescue/install media, mount the disk, chroot in, reset with passwd. Unknown SUID-root (find / -perm -4000): runs as root, so it's a privilege-escalation path or a planted backdoor shell.","Ch.6. SUID=run as owner, SGID=as group, sticky=only owner deletes (/tmp). Hard link=same inode; symlink=a path that breaks if target goes."),
("mc","A Red Hat box runs SELinux; an Ubuntu box runs AppArmor. One-line difference, and can both run at once?",
 ["SELinux = labels on everything, deny-by-default, tuned with Booleans; AppArmor = one profile per critical program; generally only one is active","They're identical and always run together","AppArmor labels everything; SELinux uses profiles","Both only log, never block"],0,
 "Ch.7: SELinux context = user:role:type:level (U aRe Totally Locked). Modes: enforcing/permissive/disabled; aa-logprof fixes an AppArmor profile from denials."),
("mc","You're told to make sure software on a Linux fleet is trustworthy. What two things matter most?",
 ["Repositories are valid (signed with trusted GPG keys) and installed files still match their package hashes (rpm -V, debsums)","Only the file names","Only that apt is used","Nothing after install"],0,
 "Ch.8: apt=Debian/Ubuntu/Mint, dnf/yum=Red Hat/Rocky, zypper=SUSE. Snap/Flatpak bundle dependencies (but publisher patches them, not the distro)."),
])]